 ## Overview
-Geometric Buffers (G-buffers) is a Non-Photorealistic Rendering (NPR) technique that decouples geometric analysis from shading by storing per-pixel geometric properties (e.g., depth, normals) in intermediate 2D images [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently generating illustrative effects (such as line drawings and edge enhancement) for complex 3D volumes by leveraging fast 2D image processing algorithms instead of expensive 3D analytic computations [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may compute shading or illumination directly in 3D space, G-buffers provide hidden surface elimination for free and allow NPR parameters to be tweaked interactively without re-traversing the volume [S1, unofficial].
